  "guideIntro": [
    "Thoreau's lecture is a defense of walking as a practice of mental and civic independence. “Wildness” names unfinished possibility, but his westward mythology also inherits settler assumptions the essay does not adequately examine."
  ],
  "notes": {
    "7": "Sauntering is given two playful origins—pilgrimage to the Holy Land and landlessness. Both detach walking from efficient transport and make it a temporary refusal of ownership.",
    "34": "The westward movement becomes national myth. Its energy is central to the essay, and so is what it erases: Indigenous presence and the violence inside “new” land imagined as open pasture.",
    "67": "When literature cannot express the Wild, Thoreau is not rejecting art; he is testing whether inherited forms can keep contact with experience that exceeds their conventions."
  },
